  {
   "id": "b2-16",
   "name": "Adaptations of seeds and fruits for dispersal"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Each method of dispersal needs special features (adaptations):\n• Wind: small and light; hairs or a parachute (kapok, Tridax); wings that spin (Combretum, mahogany). They are blown far.\n• Water: a light, fibrous, air-filled layer so the fruit floats, and a waterproof coat (coconut).\n• Animals (eaten): bright colour, scent, juicy sweet flesh; hard seed coats that pass through the gut unharmed (guava, tomato).\n• Animals (carried outside): hooks, spines or sticky surfaces (Bidens, Desmodium).\n• Self (explosive): pods with walls that dry unevenly, then twist and split, throwing the seeds (beans, castor, Bauhinia).",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "c": "a coconut"
  },
  "problem": "Explain how {c} is adapted to dispersal by water.",
  "steps": [
   "Its thick fibrous husk is full of air, so the fruit floats.",
   "Its hard waterproof shell keeps sea water out, so the seed survives long journeys and grows on a new beach."
  ],
  "answer": "Air-filled fibrous husk to float; hard waterproof shell.",
  "checks": []
 },
